From 65970839a5b0922c229bc055947765b9513fe01c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 03:40:17 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 9/19] wifi: rtl8192f-soc: bound retries and recover a stalled
 transmitter

Three error paths of the transmitter, none of which the bring-up
sequences cover:

 - Retry limits per frame type.  Broadcast frames get no retries (the
   group schedule repeats them anyway) and management frames six, instead
   of the single global retry count the previous 0x42a programming gave
   every frame type.
 - The two error latches the ISR has to acknowledge.  RXDMA_STATUS (0x288)
   is read and write-cleared alongside the TXDMA_STATUS it already was;
   a latched RX error keeps its interrupt source asserted.  TCR gets the
   error-stop pair the vendor ORs "to avoid deadlock", which for this
   part is bits 12 and 13 of the four the source writes.
 - A queue that stops being consumed.  Each ring's index register holds
   the engine's consumer index above the index this driver published, and
   those two being equal is what the vendor reads as "this queue is
   alive" (8192cd_sme.c:4148).  Sampling the consumer index twice is not
   the same test: the indices wrap with the ring, so a pass in which the
   engine took exactly a multiple of its length reads back unchanged.
   A ring whose two halves differ while its consumer index repeats for
   five passes is not being served, and a halt is declared only once no
   ring is keeping up, again as the vendor does (8192cd_sme.c:4184).
   Recovery is mac80211's restart rather than a hand-rolled engine reset,
   because this core exposes none: the HAL binds only CheckHangHandler
   (Hal8197FGen.c:299) and the vendor's own hangup path closes and reopens
   the driver with the stations copied out first (8192cd_sme.c:4716).
   Clearing TXPAUSE, re-publishing a ring's write pointer and toggling
   REG_CR's MACTXEN were each tried on a live halt and left the consumer
   index where it was.
   The restart is bounded to one per minute: a wedge that survives its own
   restart is re-declared within about twenty seconds, and unbounded that
   cadence never lets an association live long enough to carry traffic.

Why the engine stops is not reachable from anything the host can book, so
the state a halt is found in is logged before the restart: every ring's
raw index pair, the registers that could hold the transmitter without
raising an error latch, the BBP channel-loading counter - which needs no
transmit permission and therefore says whether the air was free, unlike
anything the scheduler could report about itself - a transmit-report
request, whose answer never reaches the host on this chip so that taking
it or ignoring it is a statement about the firmware alone, and the stalled
descriptor beside the last one the engine did consume.  Each of those
readings rules a candidate out without needing the halt to be
reproducible, which it is not.

The AP-side power-save boundary is recorded in a comment: mac80211 offers
no host path for answering PS-POLL, and refreshing the resident beacon on
every TIM change was measured to cost beacons rather than deliver them.

Signed-off-by: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
